# What Are Common Use Cases for Using Floats, and How Do They Work?

## What Are CSS Floats?

- **Floats** are a CSS technique originally designed to allow text to wrap around an element, such as an image.
- A floated element is moved to the:
  - Left side of its container, or
  - Right side of its container.
- Content that follows the floated element can then flow around it.

Basic syntax:

  float: left;

or:

  float: right;

---

# How Floats Work

When an element is floated:

- It is taken out of the normal document flow.
- It is pushed to the left or right of its container.
- Following content can wrap around the floated element.
- The remaining space can be filled by surrounding content.

### Example

  img {
    float: left;
  }

The image moves to the left, and text can flow around it.

---

# Classic Use Case: Text Wrapping Around Images

One of the most common uses of floats is placing an image beside text while allowing the text to wrap around it.

HTML:

  <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">

  <div class="container">
    <img src="https://placehold.co/150x150" alt="Placeholder Image">
  </div>

CSS:

  .container {
    border: 1px solid black;
    padding: 10px;
  }

  img {
    float: left;
    margin-right: 20px;
  }

### What Happens?

- The image floats to the left.
- The text can flow around the image.
- `margin-right: 20px` creates space between the image and the text.

This technique is still useful in:

- Articles
- Blogs
- News pages
- Text-heavy content

---

# Floats and the Normal Document Flow

A floated element is taken out of the normal document flow.

This can cause an important problem:

**The parent container may not automatically wrap around the floated child.**

Example:

  .container {
    border: 1px solid black;
    padding: 10px;
  }

  img {
    float: left;
    margin-right: 20px;
  }

If the image is the only child of the container, the container may collapse to almost zero height.

### Why?

The floated image is removed from the normal document flow.

The parent container therefore does not calculate its height from the floated child in the usual way.

---

# The Collapsing Parent Problem

Consider:

  <div class="container">
    <img src="https://placehold.co/150x150" alt="Placeholder Image">
  </div>

  img {
    float: left;
  }

The image is floated.

Because the image is no longer participating in normal flow, the parent may behave as though it has no normal-flow content.

This can cause:

- The parent to collapse in height.
- Borders to appear incorrectly.
- Backgrounds to not cover the floated content as expected.

---

# Floats for Multi-Column Layouts

Before Flexbox and CSS Grid became common, floats were also used to create multi-column layouts.

Developers could float multiple elements next to each other.

Example:

  .column {
    float: left;
  }

Multiple floated elements could appear horizontally, creating a column-like layout.

### Important

Today, **Flexbox** and **CSS Grid** are generally better choices for creating complex layouts.

However, floats are still important to understand because:

- Older websites may use them.
- Legacy code may contain floats.
- Some specific text-wrapping effects still use floats.

---

# The Clearfix Technique

One common solution to the collapsing parent problem is the **clearfix** technique.

Clearfix allows a parent container to properly contain its floated children.

Example:

  <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">

  <div class="container">
    <img src="https://placehold.co/150x150" alt="Placeholder Image">
    <p>This is an example of text flowing around a floated image.</p>
  </div>

CSS:

  .container {
    border: 1px solid black;
  }

  /* Clearfix CSS */
  .container::after {
    content: "";
    display: block;
    clear: both;
  }

  img {
    float: left;
    margin-right: 20px;
  }

---

# How the Clearfix Works

The clearfix uses the `::after` pseudo-element.

### 1. ::after

  .container::after

- `::after` creates a pseudo-element after the content of the container.

### 2. content

  content: "";

- Makes sure the pseudo-element is generated.
- The empty string means it does not contain visible text.

### 3. display

  display: block;

- Makes the pseudo-element a block-level element.

### 4. clear

  clear: both;

- Prevents the pseudo-element from appearing beside floated elements.
- It clears both:
  - Left floats
  - Right floats

Together, these properties create the clearfix.

---

# Complete Clearfix Example

HTML:

  <div class="container">
    <img src="https://placehold.co/150x150" alt="Placeholder Image">
    <p>
      This is an example of text flowing around a floated image.
    </p>
  </div>

CSS:

  .container {
    border: 1px solid black;
  }

  .container::after {
    content: "";
    display: block;
    clear: both;
  }

  img {
    float: left;
    margin-right: 20px;
  }

### Result

- The image floats to the left.
- The paragraph flows around the image.
- The clearfix clears the float after the content.
- The parent container properly wraps around its floated content.
- The container's border displays correctly.

---

# What Happens Without Clearfix?

If you remove:

  .container::after {
    content: "";
    display: block;
    clear: both;
  }

the parent can collapse around floated content.

This can cause the container's border or background to behave incorrectly.

---

# Why Does the Paragraph Help?

In the example:

  <div class="container">
    <img src="https://placehold.co/150x150" alt="Placeholder Image">
    <p>This is an example of text flowing around a floated image.</p>
  </div>

- The image is floated.
- The paragraph remains in the normal document flow.
- The paragraph gives the parent some normal-flow content.
- The collapse may therefore be less noticeable.

The clearfix still ensures that the parent properly contains the floated content.

---

# float Values

The main values to know are:

  float: left;

Moves the element to the left.

  float: right;

Moves the element to the right.

  float: none;

Removes floating and returns the element to its normal behavior.

---

# Float vs Modern Layout Methods

Floats were once commonly used for page layouts.

Today, modern CSS layout methods are usually preferred:

- **Flexbox** → Great for one-dimensional layouts.
- **Grid** → Great for two-dimensional layouts.
- **Float** → Still useful for text wrapping and maintaining older layouts.

### Important

Do not automatically use floats for modern complex page layouts when Flexbox or Grid is more appropriate.

